  <datafield tag="545" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">American artist Nancy Buchanan (b. 1946) began creating work in the late 1960s and early 1970s. Exploring issues of politics, commerce, environmentalism, and feminism, Buchanan has used her artwork as a tool for raising awareness of contemporary political and social issues.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="520" ind1="2" ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Fallout from the Nuclear Family is a 10-volume artwork composed of the personal and professional papers of Nancy Buchanan's father, Louis N. Ridenour, Jr. (1911-1959), bound by the artist along with her own research on the period. Ridenour, a nuclear physicist who worked on radar in the years following World War II, publicly argued that there was no defense against nuclear weapons. Buchanan, a child at the time of her father's sudden death in 1959, decided to go through his papers in 1978, and the process resulted in the creation of this artwork.The volumes contain Ridenour's original and photocopied documents and photographs, interspersed with red pages with excerpts from Buchanan's research, and blue tissue pages with excerpts from audio interviews the artist conducted with her father's associates. As a whole, Fallout from the Nuclear Family constructs a portrait of how secrecy, overzealous governmental security, growth of the military-industrial complex, and anti-Communist tactics were reflected in the life, thoughts and actions of one individual.</subfield>
  </datafield>
